\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\shape italic
Note:
\series default
 This documentation is available in PDF form from 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

http://watson.hgen.pitt.edu/docs/mega2_html/Mega2_Documentation.pdf
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and it is also included in the mega2_html folder of the Mega2 distribution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace defskip
\end_inset

During an association or linkage analysis project, one may need to analyze
 the data with several different programs.
 Unfortunately, it can often be quite difficult to get one's data in the
 proper format desired by each different computer program.
 Not only must the data be converted to the proper format, but also the
 loci must be reordered into their proper order.
 Writing custom reformatting scripts can be error-prone and very time-consuming.
 To address these problems, we created Mega2, which can be obtained following
 the installation instructions in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Installation"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To get started using Mega2, we recommend first reading the Quick Start Mega2
 mini-tutorial (see 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "sec:Quickstart---get"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mega2 can read input data in several formats: LINKAGE format, PLINK format,
 and VCF format (as listed here: 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sub:Input-formats"

\end_inset

).
 Mega2 allows one to augment these input formats with additional information,
 if desired.
 For example, trait locus pentrance information can be specified.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mega2 then takes the input files and, via a menu-driven interface, transforms
 them into various other file formats (listed here 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sub:Output-formats"

\end_inset

), thus greatly facilitating a variety of different analyses.
 In addition, for many of these options, it also sets up a C-shell script
 that then can automatically run these analyses (if you are using Mega2
 in a Unix environment that supports C-shell scripts).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mega2 is currently structured so that the user proceeds through a series
 of menus, making choices in each one (or accepting the default values),
 until the desired output files are created.
 After the desired output files are created, Mega2 exits.
 It can also be run in a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

hands-free
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 mode, using a control file to specify these choices.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In addition to the ability to reformat data for a variety of analysis programs,
 other useful features of Mega2 include:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The ability to create publication-quality PDF plots of the results using
 our nplplot R library (See section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Creating-plots"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The ability to create custom tracks of results for visualization in the
 UCSC genome browser (See section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Custom-tracks-for"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The ability to run in an automated way using batch files (See section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Batch-mode"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The availability of our Genetic Map Interpolator for aiding in constructing
 genetic maps of markers (See section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Genetic-Map-Interpolator"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The ability to simulate genotype errors (See section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sub:Simulate-errors"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Support for Mega2-annotated format files that contain informative header
 lines and are readable into R (See section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sub:Annotated-file-formats"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Support for the widely-used PLINK binary PED format (*.bed) and PLINK PED
 format (*.ped) files (See Section
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sub:PLINK-file-formats"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Support for Variant Call Format (VCF, BCF, compressed VCF) files (See Section
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sub:VCF-file-formats"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The ability to automatically zero out selected genotypes for specific individual
s in order to resolve Mendelian inconsistencies (See section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sub:Annotated-omit-file"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
In most cases, in addition to generating appropriately re-formatted files,
 Mega2 also generates a C-shell script that will automatically run the desired
 program.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Creation of a MEGA2run.html HTML summary of the most recent run of Mega2,
 with links to input and output and log files (See 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sub:MEGA2run.html-and-related"

\end_inset


\end_layout

