\begin_layout Subsection
The fast, easy way to set up your files for Mega2 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Minimal input to Mega2 consists of three matched files: (1) a names file;
 (2) a pedigree file; and (3) a map file.
 We illustrate this here with a simple example pedigree: 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename simple_ped.jpg

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This pedigree consists of the mother, father and their three offspring.
 The mother and two of the siblings are considered to be affected for a
 binary trait called 'Trait' (as indicated by the shaded symbols), and all
 five pedigree members are also genotyped at a genetic marker 'Marker1'.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To analyze this pedigree, you need to create (1) a names file containing
 the trait and marker locus names, (2) a pedigree file with relationship,
 phenotype and genotype information, and (3) a map file indicating which
 chromosome Marker1 is on and its genetic map position.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Names file:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
Mega2 supports the use of the relatively simple QTDT format "names" file
 (used by Merlin), containing two columns of data, (i) locus type and (ii)
 locus name.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Call this file names.txt .
 For our example data, this is how it looks: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
A   Trait       
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
M   Marker1
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Pedigree file: 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 The 
\shape italic
pre-makeped 
\shape default
 pedigree file format is a relatively simple format with the first 5 columns
 containing pedigree information followed by genotypes and phenotypes.
 The first 5 columns are: 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 pedigree-id, individual-id, father, mother, gender.
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
Genotypes and phenotypes have to match the order of the loci in the names
 file.
 Here, gender is coded as '1' = male, and '2' = female; the 'Trait' phenotypes
 are coded as '2' = affected, and '1' = unaffected.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Call this file pedin.txt .
 Here is how it looks for our example data: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
1   1  0  0  2  2  1  2
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
1   2  0  0  1  1  1  1 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
1   3  2  1  2  2  1  2
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
1   4  2  1  2  1  1  1
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
1   5  2  1  1  2  1  2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The columns in this file are Pedigree, Person, Father, Mother, Gender, Trait
 phenotype, Marker1 allele 1, and Marker1 allele 2.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: the 
\shape italic
pre-makeped
\shape default
 pedigree file above is exactly the same layout as a PLINK PED file; but
 while the 
\shape italic
pre-makeped
\shape default
 pedigree file may have any number of traits, the PED file may have 
\series bold
only one
\series default
 trait.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Map file: 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 This is a simple tabular format with three columns of data: chromosome
 number, position in centiMorgans and marker name.
 The first line is a header line and should be something like "Chromosome
 Haldane Name" (case is not relevant).
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
Call this file 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

map.txt
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 Note that the positions will be read in as Haldane centiMorgans.
 Our map file looks like this:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Chromosome   Haldane  Name
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
  1          1.0      Marker1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second column heading decides whether the map function is Haldane or
 Kosambi.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Convert to Mega2 annotated format files: Use the l2a.py utility to convert
 the three files you created as illustrated below: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{verbatim}
