\begin_layout Subsection
Macintosh-specific installation issues
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Introduction to Command line programs for Mac users
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You will need to familiarize yourself with using the Macintosh OS terminal,
 as installing and using Mega2 is mainly done within a Unix terminal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Unix organization conventions
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "par:Unix-organization-conventions"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The "Users" folder contains sub-folders with user names matching those that
 have user-accounts on your computer.
 Each user has full access and modification permissions to his or her /home/user
_name folder.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
All other folders can only be written or modified by the administrator.
 The /bin and /sbin folder contains system programs that are very basic
 to Unix (such as ls, cd, rm, mkdir, bash, tcsh etc.).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The /usr folder hierarchy is used to install programs that should be made
 available to all users.
 Widely-used standard Unix software such as compilers and language development
 tools (gcc, Perl, Python) are directly installed inside /usr.
 Thus to run Perl from inside a terminal, you would invoke /usr/bin/perl.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There is also a /usr/local folder hierarchy inside the /usr folder which
 is normally used to install lesser known or non-standard software, of which
 Mega2 would be an example.
 Thus, you may choose to install your genetic analysis software (such as
 Merlin, SimWalk2 etc.) inside the /usr/local/bin folder.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
The Mac OS environment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When you open up a Mac terminal window, you can use Unix commands such as
 ls (list folder contents), cd (change directory) etc.
 The default folder associated with such a window (i.e.
 before you issue a a cd command), is your "home" folder, generally named
 
\emph on
/Users/your-user-name.

\emph default
 Your Desktop folder is called 
\emph on
/Users/your-user-name/Desktop
\emph default
.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Command line version of R
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
R is required to use Mega2’s Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium estimation options,
 as well as for plotting LOD scores from the output of Merlin, SimWalk2,
 and Allegro.
 Therefore, for full functionality, you need to obtain and install the R
 software.
 The command line version of R is identical to R as used on other Unix operating
 systems.
 Therefore general documentation for R applies to this version as well.
 On each release (and patched-release), binaries are distributed through
 CRAN.
 These binaries come with a common installer used by R.app so please read
 the related notes (see How to get R.app).
 To use R, you probably need to add a symbolic link on your system as the
 R binary is located inside the MAC 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

framework
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 hierarchy.
 Suppose you have the /usr/local/bin directory on your system (if you do
 not have one, you can use /usr/bin instead), you should just type in your
 Terminal (a root password is required) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
sudo ln -s /Library/Frameworks/R.framework/Resources/R /usr/local/bin/R
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assuming that you have /usr/local/bin in your PATH environment variable,
 you will be able to launch R from any location on your system just by typing
 R.
 In this way, when you install a new version of the R.framework this link
 will point to the latest R binary.
\end_layout

