\begin_layout Subsection
Mega2 input file formats
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sub:Annotated-file-formats"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mega2 can read input file sets in the Mega2 format (previously referred
 to as the Annotated format), which consist of files are in table format
 with a header line defining the names of each data column.
 Columns are separated by one or more white-space characters (tabs or blanks),
 and rows are separated by newlines (or returns).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Some columns require special reserved names (these are listed as fixed names
 below under each file heading), and other column names are specified by
 the user following our naming convention.
 Every file has some required columns (e.g.
 pedigree file always has an ID column), and some files allow optional columns,
 including extra ones that are ignored by Mega2.
 A column is read in and ignored if its name contains 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

X.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 as the first two characters.
 Note that the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

#
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 character at the beginning of the column name also works, but the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

X.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 convention allows files to be compatible with R.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Data lines below the header column are read in accordance with the column
 names/types, therefore, there is no special order required for the columns
 themselves, as is the case for the linkage format files.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Mega2 distribution package contains a conversion program for converting
 from the old LINKAGE-based format files into the Mega2 annotated format
 files (See section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Converting-to-annotated-format"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mega2 requires three matched files as its input.
 These are the locus, pedigree, and map data files.
 This trio of files can be supplemented by an omit file, for omitting specific
 data points from all reformatted output files.
 It is easiest if you give these files their 
\series bold
default names
\series default
 with the same extension, as then Mega2 will automatically fill in the file
 names for you when you specify the chromosome number.
 So, for example, if your files contained information for just chromosome
 4 markers, then it is easiest if you name them as follows: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
  datain.04
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
  pedin.04
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
  map.04
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
  omit.04
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mega2 also supports two more optional files: an allele-frequency file, and
 a penetrance file for affection status traits.
 The default file names are (e.g.
 for chromosome 4): 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
  frequency.04 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
  penetrance.04
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the current version, there is no provision for providing quantitative
 trait distribution parameters.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\begin_inset VSpace defskip
\end_inset

WINDOWS/DOS USERS WARNING
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If you are creating your files on a Windows or DOS system and then transferring
 them to a Unix machine, please remember to convert the DOS end-of-line
 characters to Unix end-of-line characters.
 When run on a Unix machine, Mega2 will detect DOS end-of-line characters
 and terminate with an error.
 See the trouble-shooting section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sub:DOS-format-file-related"

\end_inset

