\begin_layout Paragraph

\series bold
Special chromosome names and numbers 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
X chromosome
\series default
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 
\series bold
 Annotated format 
\series default
 - 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

X
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 denotes x-linked loci in the annotated format map file.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 
\series bold
Non-annotated format 
\series default
 - 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

23
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 should be used as the chromosome label to denote X-linked loci in the non-annot
ated map file.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Females can be heterozygous at X-linked markers, whereas males should be
 assigned homozygous genotypes.
 Males' genotypes are then analyzed as being hemizygous by Mega2 and the
 other genetic analysis software that can handle X-linked loci.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mega2 asks the user if loci on chromosome 23 (in non-annotated format input
 files) should be considered as X-linked or autosomal.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Pseudo-X region of X and Y chromosomes
\series default
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 
\series bold
 Annotated format 
\series default
 - Some genes are common to both X and Y chromosomes in humans.
 These genes are said to reside in the pseudo-autosomal region.
 The chromosome labels should be set to 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

XY
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 
\series bold
 Non-annotated format 
\series default
 - In non-annotated format files, such markers should be labeled with chromosome
 number 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

25
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Loci labeled as pseudo-autosomal are treated as autosomal loci.
 (Before Mega2 4.5.5 this used to be 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

24
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Y chromosome
\series default
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 
\series bold
 Annotated format 
\series default
 - 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Y
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 should be used as the chromosome label for markers that are to be treated
 as Y-linked.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 
\series bold
 Non-annotated format 
\series default
 - 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

24
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 should be the chromosome number inside the non-annotated format map file.
 (Before Mega2 4.5.5 this used to be 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

25
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.)
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Note that most analysis software listed inside our output options do not
 handle Y-linked loci.
 We have included this facility so that the user can check and clean data
 for Y-linked loci as well as generate allele-frequency and genotyping summaries.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Females' genotypes for Y-linked loci are ignored for now, and males are
 expected to be homozygous at these loci.
 In future, we will provide counts on 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

putative
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 female genotypes at Y-linked loci, in order to flag data errors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Mitochondrial chromosome
\series default
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 
\series bold
 Annotated format 
\series default
 - 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

MT
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 should be used as the chromosome label for markers that are to be treated
 as linked to the mitochondrial chromosomes.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 
\series bold
 Non-annotated format 
\series default
 - 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

26
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 should be the chromosome number for mitochondrial markers.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Although an individual is expected to be homozygous at mitochondrial markers,
 and also have inherited the mother's genotype exclusively, there have been
 known to be deviations from this inheritance pattern.
 Therefore, for the present, Mega2 only reports genotypes which are heterozygous
, or different from the maternal genotype, it does not flag these as Mendelian
 inheritance errors, nor are these reset along with other Mendelian inconsistenc
ies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Unknown chromosome
\series default
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 If the input data contains unmapped markers, some limited analysis is still
 possible on such markers, which do not need map information.
 These include the allele-frequency estimation and data-checking/cleaning
 steps of Mega2, as well as marker-related summaries.
 Some output options do not need map information.
 These are: SUMMARY options, PLINK (using a map file with dummy positions),
 the HWE options, which do not need a genetic map, LINKAGE, NUCLEAR PEDIGREES,
 PREMAKEPED-FORMAT, and PREST.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 
\series bold
 Annotated format 
\series default
 - Use the label 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

U
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 inside the annotated format map file to label unmapped markers.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\series bold
 Non-annotated format 
\series default
 - Use 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

999
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 to label unmapped markers inside the non-annotated format map file.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Output file labels
\series default
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

If input files were in annotated format with chromosomes labeled with X,Y
 etc.
 the output files also have character extensions instead of chromosome numbers
 (e.g.
 Lpedin.X, Ldatain.X etc.).
 If input files were in non-annotated format, numeric extensions are used,
 23-25 and 999 for the unknown chromosome.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
New reordering menu items
\series default

\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 For analysis menu options that can handle unmapped loci, you will be able
 to select the unknown chromosome as well as the usual numbered chromosomes
 if your map file contains such loci.
 The menu option which allows selection of multiple chromosomes for output
 now offers 3 choices:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 1) All known chromosomes.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 2) Specific known chromosomes.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 3) All mapped and unmapped loci.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Annotated omit file
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sub:Annotated-omit-file"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The annotated omit file contains the three required columns as in the case
 of non-annotated files.
 Again, names must be maintained, ordering of columns doesn't matter, and
 neither does the case in the header line.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Pedigree  Individual  Marker
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
1 All All
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
2 10  M2
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
2 All M1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The optional omit file permits one to easily delete certain marker genotypes
 from all Mega2-generated output files.
 This is useful if certain marker genotypes are Mendelianly-inconsistent,
 yet one wants to preserve the original marker data in the input file.
 Marker genotypes can be omitted for a whole family at once or for one specific
 individual.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The omit file should be in the following format:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each line should have two integers and a string, separated by white space.
 The first number is the pedigree number and must match that used in your
 input LINKAGE format file.
 The second number is the person number and must match that used in your
 input LINKAGE format file.
 The string should be either All or the name of the locus.
 If All is used, the person or pedigree indicated will be untyped at all
 marker loci.
 Trait phenotypes will not be set to unknown.
 Specific omit file entries containing the name of the trait locus should
 be included to do the latter.
 If the person number is zero, then all marker genotypes will be set to
 unknown for the entire pedigree.
 Otherwise only the indicated person will be untyped.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A summary of the omit results will be found in the file omit.log.
 This file is rewritten the next time MEGA2 is run with an omit file specified.
 If MEGA2 can not find a person or pedigree as specified in the input omit
 file, it will halt with an error message.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Example omit file, 'omit.05':
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Pedigree  Individual  Marker
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
1 0 All
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
2 10 M2
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
2 0  M1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This file generates the following log file: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Marker untyped everyone in pedigree 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Marker untyped pedigree 2 person 10 at locus M2
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Marker untyped everyone in pedigree 2 at locus M1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The omit file can now be used to set trait phenotypes to unknown.
 Here is such an example: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Pedigree  Individual  Marker
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
1 11 AFF2
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
1 11 QUANT1
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These two lines direct Mega2 to untype person 11 of pedigree 1 at the affection
 locus AFF2 and at the QTL trait QUANT1.
 The affection status will be set to unknown (0), and the quantitative phenotype
 will be set to the appropriate missing value in the output.
 These actions are logged as well.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Please note that when the marker column contains the keyword 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

All
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, it still refers to only marker loci, trait loci are left untouched.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Hint: See the section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sub:omit-file"

\end_inset

 on creating omit files based on errors found by running the pedigree checking
 program Pedcheck.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Annotated frequency file
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sub:Annotated-frequency-file"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The annotated frequency file contains one line for each marker and allele.
 All alleles present in the data have to be listed.
 The allele labels should match those in the pedigree file (raw or coded).
 The column headings should be as listed below (letter case and order of
 columns don't matter).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Name Allele Frequency
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
TRAIT 1 0.990000
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
TRAIT 2 0.010000
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Q1 1 0.990000
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Q1 2 0.010000
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
M1 1 0.500000
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
M1 2 0.500000
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
M3 1 0.250000
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
M3 2 0.250000
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
M3 3 0.250000
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
M3 4 0.250000
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
M2 1 0.500000
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
M2 2 0.500000
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is not necessary to have all your loci listed within the frequency file,
 however, if a locus is included, it is necessary to list ALL its alleles.
 If this is not so, then alleles encountered in the genotype data, and not
 specified inside the frequency file will be flagged as errors.
 Locus names listed inside the frequency file, but missing from the names
 file will cause Mega2 to terminate with error messages.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For marker loci whose allele frequencies are not listed, Mega2 estimates
 them, as well as recoding them to consecutive integer values.
 For trait loci, Mega2 accepts only 2 alleles.
 If trait allele frequencies are not provided, then Mega2 uses default values
 of 0.5 for both alleles.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Annotated penetrance file
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sub:Annotated-penetrance-file"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Affection status traits are listed in the penetrance file.
 This file should contain 5 columns listing the name class number and penetrance
 values for the three genotypes in each class.
 As in the names file and frequency file a header is required; the column
 headers should be as specified below (case is ignored, and there is no
 special ordering).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Name   Class  Pen.11    Pen.12    Pen.22
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
TRAIT 1 0.0000 1.0000 1.0000
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With the above header, the penetrances are assumed to be autosomal.
 An additional column, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Type
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

, may be added to the header with row value of 
\emph on
autosomal
\emph default
 for autosomal chromosomes or with the values, 
\emph on
female
\emph default
, or 
\emph on
male
\emph default
 to indicate the penetrance values for the particular population on the
 X chromosome.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
Name   Class  Pen.11    Pen.12    Pen.22 Type
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
TRAIT 1 0.1000 1.0000 1.0000 autosomal
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
TRAIT 1 0.1500 1.0000 1.0000 female
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
TRAIT 1 0.0500 1.0000 1.0000 male
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
NOTE: Every row must have 6 column, even though there is no meaningful value
 for Pen.12 for males.
 Whatever value is put in the Pen.12 column is ignored.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
PLINK input file formats
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sub:PLINK-file-formats"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The PLINK program (
\begin_inset Flex URL
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

http://pngu.mgh.harvard.edu/~purcell/plink/
\end_layout

\end_inset

) written by Shaun Purcell and colleagues has become a 
\shape italic
de facto
\shape default
 standard for whole genome association analysis.
 It uses input files that contain the same kind of information as the Mega2
 annotated format.
 It also allows for several different formats of the input data and supports
 conversion between these formats.
 Perhaps the most popular (but unreadable) format is its compressed binary
 PED format.
 Mega2 supports the binary format and one of the text formats, PED.
 We suggest you visit the PLINK documentation web site (
\begin_inset Flex URL
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

http://pngu.mgh.harvard.edu/~purcell/plink/
\end_layout

\end_inset

) to understand these formats.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our desire is to have Mega2 accept the PLINK files unmodified.
 One 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

feature
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of the PLINK file format is that some of the columns in the files listed
 below may be omitted by using a corresponding flag when starting PLINK.
 Mega2 will accept most of those flags and interpret them as the PLINK program
 would.
 The flags are explained below.
 How to enter these flags is explained in section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sub:PLINK-parameters"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To instruct Mega2 to read PLINK format files, choose options 12 or 13 in
 the initial Mega2 input menu:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
12) Switch to PLINK input menu (binary format)
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
13) Switch to PLINK input menu (ped format)
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code

\end_layout

