\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The LINKAGE format is essentially the de facto standard for coding pedigree
 information in a machine-readable form.
 For a complete description of this format, please see the Handbook of Human
 Genetic Linkage (Terwilliger and Ott 1994) and the LINKAGE Users Guide
 (at 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

http://linkage.rockefeller.edu/soft/linkage/
\end_layout

\end_inset

) .
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 pre-Makeped LINKAGE pedigree file consist of columns of integer data.
 The pre-Makeped columns are: 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{verbatim}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Pedigree Person Father Mother Gender Phenotype1 Phenotype2 Phenotype3 ...
 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{verbatim}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where missing parents are entered as 0 (zero), and, for the gender column,
 a 1 = Male and a 2 = Female (This is easy to remember if you think of the
 number of X chromosomes).
 Makeped inserts some additional columns of pointers (which would be difficult
 to enter by hand) and breaks loops, which is required by the LINKAGE programs.Th
e columns should be separated by spaces or tabs (any number of these is
 allowed).
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 While the order of the phenotypes is arbitrary, it is common to put the
 affection status phenotype first, followed by the marker phenotypes (which,
 for co-dominant markers, are the same as the genotypes).
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 
\series bold
Phenotype coding: 
\series default

\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 1) 
\series bold
Trait locus:
\series default
 To code a simple affection status locus, use these codes: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
0 = unknown
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
1 = normal
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
2 = affected
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
2) 
\series bold
Marker locus:
\series default
 To code a codominant marker locus phenotype, simply list the two numbered
 alleles with at least one space or tab between the alleles.
 The unknown genotype is coded as 0 0.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Using non-numeric allele names: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Non-numeric allele names are allowed inside the pedigree file only with
 the use of a names file, and only for numbered marker loci.
 The recoded output pedigrees will have their genotypes altered to numeric
 alleles if the target output format requires this.
 Allele names have to be strings, and may not contain white-space characters,
 since the pedigree file is read in as a white-space separated column format.
 See the names file section above for details on recoding.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: Everyone must have either two parents or no parents in the data set.
 Thus, to connect relatives one may need to include people in a pedigree
 for whom there is at present no data.
 Example of post-Makeped file corresponding to the pre-Makeped file (above):
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code

\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
   1   1   0   0   4   0   0 1  1  2  21.2 1  2  1  2  1  1
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
   1   2   0   0   4   0   0 2  0  0   1.3 1  2  1  1  2  2
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
   1   3   0   0   7   0   0 1  0  0   0.9 2  2  1  2  2  1
