\begin_layout Subsection
Locus reordering option 1) Select all loci in map order on chromosome.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Option 1 is used to select a single chromosome.
 By default, it is set to the first chromosome encountered in the input
 data.
 If the user selects this item, then the user is prompted to enter a new
 chromosome number.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If the user hits 0 at this point to proceed, then loci present on this chromosom
e will be included in the output file, arranged as per their map positions
 as specified by the input map file.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Locus reordering option 2) Select by locus number.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Option 2 permits the user to select a set of loci from a numbered list by
 entering their numbers in the desired order.
 The user may at any time during the selection process type in the character
 ‘v’ to review the original loci list or ‘o’ to view all the loci he/she
 has selected.
 To finalize the selection process the user will include an ‘e’ at the end
 of the string or on the next input line (e.g.
 ‘1 3 8-11 e’ is a typical input string and will select the loci at the
 1, 3, 8 through 11th positions in the original locus order).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In previous versions of Mega2, during the display of loci via the 'v' input,
 the user had to type 'q' to end the display, then enter locus numbers.
 In the new version of Mega2, instead of typing 'q', locus numbers can be
 entered followed by the carriage-return.
 This will save the selections from the current screen, then continue onto
 the display of the next screen-full of loci.
 This is useful if selections have to be made from different regions on
 the chromosome, out of many loci, as is often the case with SNP data.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 HINT: 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Several of the output options will complain if markers are not ordered
 according to their map positions, so this has to be kept track of while
 re-ordering the loci manually.
\end_layout

