\begin_layout Paragraph
Chromosome_Single 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This option refers to the locus reordering menu option 1.
 It refers to the chromosome selected for analysis.
 If item 
\series bold
12
\series default
 below is specified as well, then the marker numbers specified by 
\series bold
12
\series default
 refer to markers on the chromosome specified by this option.
 The value should be a single integer.
 The validity of the chromosome is decided after the locus data file and
 map file are read in, and the chromosome numbers present within the input
 data are known.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 If there are no chromosomal markers, then this item is ignored, and the
 user is required to provide loci numbers using the Loci_Selected keyword.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Chromosomes_Multiple_Num 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "par:Chromosomes_Multiple_Num"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This option is a single integer which decides how many chromosome numbers
 should be read in option 11.
 Valid values are positive integers.
 This option is necessary for option Chromosomes_Multiple
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "par:Chromosomes_Multiple"

\end_inset

 to work, otherwise Mega2 will report an error in the batch-file and terminate.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Chromosomes_Multiple 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "par:Chromosomes_Multiple"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This option specifies which chromosomes should be selected for output.
 It should be a list of integers, each being a chromosome number present
 in the input data (as decided by the map and locus data files).
 In addition to integers the values 'X', 'Y', 'XY', and 'MT' are also valid.
 This option also requires that the batch file option Chromosomes_Multiple_Num
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "par:Chromosomes_Multiple_Num"

\end_inset

 be specified.
 There must be at least as many chromosome numbers in this list as specified
 in option Chromosomes_Multiple_Num.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the three options 8-10, if option 8 is specified, the others are ignored.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Loop_Over_Chromosomes
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "par:Loop_Over_Chromosomes"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This option specifies whether data from multiple chromosomes are written
 to the same file or not.
 If true (y), each chromosome gets its own file.
 If false (n) only one file is produced which contains data for all chromosomes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Loci_Selected 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Important: From version 3.0 onwards, the keyword Markers_Selected_Num has
 been decommissioned, and the keyword Markers_Selected is now Loci_Selected.
 If the old keywords appear within the batch file, they will be ignored.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 The Loci_Selected option specifies the marker numbers to be selected for
 analysis.
 As mentioned in option 9, these markers refer to the ones present on the
 chromosome number specified in option 9.
 Whether these markers are valid can only be decided after marker data has
 been read in.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 The list of markers have to be terminated with 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

e
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Value_Genetic_Distance_Index 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "par:Map_Index"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This item specifies which genetic map to use for analysis.
 The index is zero based.
 Maps are numbered in the order that they are first discovered in the map
 file, reading from left to right.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As an example, if the input map file is that discussed in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sub:Annotated-map-file"

\end_inset

, then map ‘M’ would be represented by an index value of 0, map ‘M2’ by
 an index value of 1, map ‘M3’ by an index value of 2, and map ‘M4’ by an
 index value of 3.
 Since only map ‘M’ and ‘M4’ are genetic maps, valid values for the 'Value_Genet
ic_Distance_Index' are 0 and 3.
\end_layout

