\begin_layout Subsection
Hints on loci reordering
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If you wish to create a set of LINKAGE format files with all the markers
 re-arranged into their map order, one chromosome after the other, choose
 Option ‘18) Convert to Linkage format’ from the Analysis menu and then
 choose the sub-option 1 ‘Select all markers in order on all chromosomes
 from Option 4 of the Locus Reordering Menu.
 This is useful, for example, if you want to run the Analyze package and
 you want all the markers in chromosomal order.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If you have a single affection status locus in your input files, and then
 choose sub-option 1 from Option 4 of the Locus Reordering Menu, then Mega2
 will create multiple sets of chromosome-specific files for many of the
 analysis options, including ASPEX, GeneHunter-Plus, and GeneHunter.
 A ‘global’ C-shell script will be created that will analyze one chromosome
 after another, all at once.
 This greatly eases analyses of genome-wide data sets.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Option 4 lets the user select some or all chromosomes.
 Typically the output files are chromosome-specific, each set of output
 files containing genotype data from all marker loci on one chromosome along
 with trait loci.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Many linkage analysis programs do not care about the ordering of trait
 loci with respect to the marker.
 So, by default Mega2 would place the trait loci at the beginning of the
 marker order.
 If this order is important to you, then there are two ways to specify an
 alternate ordering: 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Reorder menu option 2: This works for markers on a single chromosome.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Trait selection menu's Combine option 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 If menu item number 2 is not set to the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Combine
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 mode, this should be the first step BEFORE selecting the trait loci.
 Enter 2 at the prompt to switch modes, then press 1 to obtain a list of
 traits along with a [MARKER] item, which is a place-holder for marker loci.Speci
fy the desired order using the item numbers.
 The resulting output file will have the markers inserted in the right place
 with respect to the trait loci.
 For chromosome-specific output files, markers for each chromosome will
 be substituted in turn, otherwise, if chromosomes are being combined into
 one set of files, then all chromosomal markers selected appear in this
 position.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsection
Problems commonly encountered with input files:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Fatal errors cause Mega2 to terminate without creating output files.
 We try to identify as many problems with the data files as we can before
 termination of a Mega2 run, for the user's benefit.
 However, several iterations may be necessary with wrong or corrupted data.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Pedigree file reading problem 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Reading in a pre-makeped file as a post-makeped file has been reported frequentl
y in the past.
 Mega2 judges whether a file is in pre-makeped format or post-makeped format
 by looking at the 5th column, which should be the 
\emph on
sex
\emph default
 column in a pre-makeped file format.
 If this column contains anything other than 1s and 2s, the pedigree file
 is read in as a post-makeped file, so either the sex column has to be filled
 in, or the pedigrees need to be converted to post-makeped outside of Mega2.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Mega2 hangs while reading in input files
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A common cause is that one of the files do not have a newline at the end
 of the last line.
 An easy way to check this on a Unix system is to count the lines using
 the command 
\series bold

\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

wc -l
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\series default
 .
 If the last line does not contain a newline, then this will print a number
 which is one short of the expected number of lines.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
DOS format file related errors
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sub:DOS-format-file-related"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The most common problem users have faced so far has had to do with DOS format
 map files.
 DOS text files contain the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

^M
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 carriage-return character in addition to the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

^J
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 line-feed character used by the Unix C library routines.
 This caused the map file to be read in incorrectly by earlier versions
 up to version 2.1 beta R3.
 This resulted in warning messages such as: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
WARNING: Locus GENE1 is not in map file.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
although GENE1 is actually present in the marker file.
 In extreme cases, Mega2 cannot find a single marker defined in the Map
 file and terminates abnormally.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This problem is only encountered if DOS-files are being run though a Unix
 copy of Mega2.
 Therefore, each input file is first checked for 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

^M
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 characters, if these are present, Mega2 exits with an error message.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Dos formatted files should be converted to Unix formatted files by removing
 the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

^M
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 characters.
 This can be done manually, or through freely available programs.
 If you have Perl installed, here is a one-line Perl utility: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
perl -pi -e 's/
\backslash
r
\backslash
n/
\backslash
n/g' pedin.01
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Solaris usually comes with a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

dos2unix
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 utility installed.
 Linux RPMs are available for a variety of platforms (try to locate one
 suitable for you.) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Files created by Mac-specific applications may contain the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

^M
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 character, but not the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

^J
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 The 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

dos2unix
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

mac2unix
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 utilities may be used to convert these files, alternatively, you can use
 Perl: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
perl -pi -e 's/
\backslash
r/
\backslash
n/g' pedin.01
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For more information, see 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newline#Conversion_utilities
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

