\begin_layout Subsection
Set up for TDTMax analyses
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This option permits easy setup of files for the ‘tdtmax’ program from this
 paper: 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Ann Hum Genet 1997 Jan;61( Pt 1):49-60 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Randomization tests of disease-marker associations.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Morris AP, Curnow RN, Whittaker JC
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 This approach is very nice in that it uses randomization approach to correct
 for the problem of multiple testing (across alleles) inherent to the TDT
 approach for testing for association.
 However, Dr.
 Morris seems not to be distributing his program anymore, and so now the
 ASPEX program may be used instead, as the ASPEX program now uses randomization
 in a similar way to compute appropriately corrected empirical p-values.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 The shell scripts generated assume that you have the ‘convert’ and ‘tdtmax’
 programs installed and in your path.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 The TDTMAX option will create a plethora of files, one per marker, with
 the default name “tdtmax_data.#.locus_name”, where “#” is the chromosome
 number and “locus_name” is the name of the marker.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 It will also create a C-shell script with the default name “tdtmax#.sh”,
 where “#” is the chromosome number.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 You also have to choose between these two analysis options.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 1) all affected offspring in families
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 2) only one affected sib per family
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Please see the ‘tdtmax’ documentation for a discussion of these options.
 However, suffice it to say here that the “all affected offspring in families”
 uses transmitted and non-transmitted alleles from all affected offspring.
 The authors of tdtmax advise against this option, and instead recommend
 that one uses only one affected sib per family (NOTE: the tdtmax documentation
 does not seem to indicate how this one sib is selected.).
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 You also need to enter the number of permutations desired.
 Obviously, one should use as many permutations as is computationally feasible.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Output from the ‘tdtmax#.sh’ C-shell script file:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 The C-shell script file will, if all goes well, create (or append to) two
 output files:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 a) tdtmax.lst - This file contains the complete output generated by the
 ‘tdtmax’ program.
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 b) tdtmax.sum - This file contains summary output, one line per marker,
 giving the marker name, the position on the chromosome, the chromosome
 number, and the empirical tdtmax p-value.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

