\begin_layout Subsection
Convert to PANGAEA MORGAN format 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sub:Detailed-info-Pangaea-Morgan"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

MORGAN (Monte Carlo Genetic Analysis) is a collection of programs and libraries
 developed at the University of Washington under the PANGAEA (Pedigree Analysis
 for Genetics and Epidemiological Attributes) umbrella.
 This software implements a number of methods for the analysis of data observed
 on members of a pedigree, with the main programs implementing Markov Chain
 Monte Carlo (MCMC) methods.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (Taken from the tutorial introduction.) The 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Morgan tutorial
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 is available at 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

http://www.stat.washington.edu/thompson/Stuff_bk/morgan-tut.pdf
\end_layout

\end_inset

; there are also example data files that are provided to match the tutorial.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The software download is available via 
\begin_inset Flex URL
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

http://www.stat.washington.edu/thompson/Genepi/MORGAN/Morgan.shtml
\end_layout

\end_inset

 We have used version 3.1.1 in our Mega2 development work.
 You should 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\emph on
untar
\emph default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 the release file, 
\series bold
morgan311_release.tgz,
\series default
 (in our case) into a convenient directory and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\emph on
make
\emph default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 the software.
 The are several 
\emph on
README
\emph default
 files that explain more of the details of 
\series bold
MORGAN
\series default
.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One very useful thing to note is that in each subdirectory that contains
 one or more executable programs (as opposed to libraries), there is a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\emph on
Gold
\emph default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 subdirectory that contains tests for the programs.
 By necessity, these 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\emph on
Gold
\emph default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 directories also contain sample parameter files for these programs.
 In addition, the directory that contains the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Gold
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 subdirectory also contains a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

README_userdoc
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 file that explains the programs and shows all the possible parameters for
 the programs.
 There are many dozens of possible options that can be present in a parameter
 file.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
All of the 
\series bold
MORGAN
\series default
 programs accept the same format data files: a pedigree file, a marker file,
 and a parameter file; Mega2 will produce these files for you.
 We have chosen several representative 
\series bold
MORGAN
\series default
 programs to produce data and parameter files for rather than for all the
 
\series bold
MORGAN
\series default
 programs: we support: 
\emph on
pedcheck
\emph default
, 
\emph on
kin
\emph default
, 
\emph on
translink
\emph default
, 
\emph on
lm_linkage
\emph default
, 
\emph on
lm_bayes
\emph default
, and two versions of 
\emph on
lm_ibdtests
\emph default
 one using likelihood-ratios and the other not.
 Our design is intended to make it easy for a user to take one of these
 existing examples and just replace the program to be run to get different
 
\series bold
MORGAN
\series default
 functionality.
 We also have split the parameter files into two subfiles.
 One that refers to the pedigree and marker data and the other has the parameter
s for the desired program to be run.
 We do not expect to have specified all the parameters that someone might
 want in all cases and expect that the user will change and customize the
 parameters as needed.
 We have chosen default parameter values based on the examples provided
 in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Gold
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 directories of the 
\series bold
MORGAN
\series default
 distribution - the user should carefully tune the parameter values to their
 particular data set and desired analysis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We provide shell scripts to run 
\series bold
MORGAN
\series default
 programs for all the traits and chromosome selected by Mega2.
 These scripts must find the appropriate program.
 We require that the environment variable 
\series bold
MORGAN_RELEASE
\series default
 be set to the directory that 
\series bold
MORGAN
\series default
 release was 
\emph on
untared
\emph default
 into.
 Please see the section on 
\series bold
Beagle
\series default
 (below) for a discussion of several strategies for setting an environment
 variable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One final note: In principle, 
\series bold
MORGAN
\series default
 allows a single marker file to have data from different chromosomes.
 But not all the 
\series bold
MORGAN
\series default
 programs support this extension.
 We have chosen not to implement the feature.
 So all output generated by Mega2 will have a separate marker file for each
 chromosome.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Convert to Beagle format
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sub:Detailed-info-Beagle"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The following chromosome specific files are created for Beagle:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
BEAGLE shell top file:      beagle.all.sh
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code

\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
BEAGLE bp marker file:      beagle.01.pmrk.gz
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
or
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
BEAGLE gd marker file:      beagle.01.gmrk.gz
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code

\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
BEAGLE genotype file:       beagle.01.bgl.gz
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
BEAGLE shell file:          beagle.01.sh
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Beagle is used for imputing genotypes, inferring haplotype phase, and performing
 genetic association analysis.
 Since Beagle can read gzipped files, the marker and genotype files are
 output in gzip format for those platforms that support it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Important:
\series default
 Note that to get correct results when converting to Beagle, your input
 data must contain the appropriate data.
 If you wish to set up data in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

unphased unrelated
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Beagle format, then your input data must consist of unrelated case/control
 individuals.
 If you wish to set up data in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

unphased trio
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Beagle format, then your input data must consist of parent-child trio families
 with unaffected parents and affected children.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mega2 supports three of the valid Beagle genotype files (unphased unrelated,
 unphased trio, and unphased pair) through the sub-analysis options for
 Beagle (See 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "Beagle"

\end_inset

).
 Beagle marker files can contain either physical or genetic map information.
 Both genetic (.gmrk.gz) and physical (.pmrk.gz) map files will be output if
 the maps are available on input.
 However, if both physical and genetic maps exist, the genetic map will
 be used by the shell file produced by Mega2 .
 You may change this by editing the shell file.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Beagle is written in Java and is distributed in the form of a Java ARchive
 (.jar) file as 'beagle.jar'.
 The shell script produced by Mega2 assumes that the Beagle .jar file is
 referenced by the shell environment variable BEAGLE_JAR (which defaults
 to a file named 'beagle.jar' in the current directory).
 The shell script will generate an error if the .jar file is not found.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here are some examples of how to set the BEAGLE_JAR environment variable
 so that the shell script may locate the beagle .jar file in a different
 directory other than the current directory.
 These examples assume that you have installed the Beagle distribution at
 the location '/usr/local/src/beagle'.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If you are using a Bash or Korn shell, then you would use:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
export BEAGLE_JAR=/usr/local/src/beagle/beagle.jar 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If you are using a C shell (csh), then you would use:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
setenv BEAGLE_JAR /usr/local/src/beagle/beagle.jar
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If you are using using Windows, then the setting of the environment variable
 is implementation dependent.
 It is generally done through the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

User variables
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 section of the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Environment Variables
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 button located on the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Advanced
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 tab of the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

System Properties
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Control Panel
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 item.
 Once it is set it is retained when the user logs out and in again.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In practice the environment variable should be placed in a file to be defined
 once on user login.
 This file is dependent on the shell that you are using.
 For the Bash shell it is the user's .bash_profie file; for the C shell it
 is the user's .login file; for the Korn shell it is the user's .profile file.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If you encounter a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Java heap space
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 exception while running Beagle, please consult the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Memory Management
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 section of the Beagle documentation for further assistance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Please note that Unphased pair and trio data is not permitted to have any
 Mendelian inconsistencies.
 Genotypes causing Mendelian inconsistencies for a trio must be replaced
 with missing genotypes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Please also note that Loop_Over_Chromosomes = y is assumed when converting
 to Beagle format files (See 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "par:Loop_Over_Chromosomes"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The genetic map output for Beagle will be in Haldane cM.
\end_layout

